{"h":3396,"lines":[{"x":0.098530865941945975,"h":0.015561567740108972,"c":1,"t":"Test 2","y":0.030009913667988886,"w":0.062197526295979813},{"c":1,"t":"What are we to do, then, when confronted with situations like a disagreement with a spouse or","w":0.80893880208333324,"x":0.096554480045041094,"y":0.077624670247021577,"h":0.021595142755407304},{"x":0.096555963606823614,"t":"negotiating a contract at work, that require us to take a personal stake? Grossmann argues that","y":0.095309213594127584,"w":0.8068899536132812,"c":1,"h":0.021426783854885501},{"h":0.020433252916459588,"c":1,"t":"even when we aren't able to change the situation, we can still evaluate these experiences from","w":0.80057922363281242,"x":0.096626289914976654,"y":0.114352402896709},{"x":0.096395853348269139,"t":"different perspectives.","y":0.13068789215319332,"w":0.19196034749348959,"h":0.018747014067057921,"c":1},{"y":0.16508830311061296,"w":0.81933675130208317,"h":0.02394168031510524,"c":1,"x":0.096498716383453503,"t":"For example, in one experiment that took place during the peak of a recent economic recession,"},{"c":1,"h":0.020808459170715499,"w":0.7636257934570313,"y":0.18438419955959473,"t":"graduating college seniors were asked to reflect on their job prospects. The students were","x":0.094539126385634137},{"h":0.022007195210990371,"c":1,"x":0.09452605477351976,"y":0.2007331428652217,"w":0.80891108194986983,"t":"instructed to imagine their career either 'as if you were a distant observer' or 'before your own"},{"h":0.024020092787815694,"w":0.81307586669921872,"c":1,"t":"eyes as if you were right there. Participants in the group assigned to the 'distant observer' role","y":0.21795890972070631,"x":0.0944955536804514},{"x":0.094472162036910279,"c":1,"w":0.82338165283203124,"y":0.23743914939247945,"h":0.022453867504537817,"t":"displayed more wisdom-related reasoning (intellectual humility and recognition of change) than"},{"x":0.0944658947670384,"w":0.31930597941080729,"t":"did participants in the control group.","y":0.2548627767301952,"c":1,"h":0.018776079229527953},{"y":0.29028762951683773,"h":0.021426784978176849,"t":"In another study, couples in long-term romantic relationships were instructed to visualize","w":0.7657430521647135,"x":0.092435639609725326,"c":1},{"x":0.094520098803947683,"w":0.77598424275716149,"h":0.020284085447852873,"y":0.30871362590249496,"t":"an unresolved relationship conflict either through the eyes of an outsider or from their own","c":1},{"c":1,"y":0.32457033446409067,"t":"perspective. Participants then discussed the incident with their partner for 10 minutes, after which","x":0.092470039899953824,"w":0.83775044759114592,"h":0.023482063212580062},{"h":0.022790584182289964,"y":0.3425527109302211,"c":1,"x":0.094482242297743546,"t":"they wrote down their thoughts about it. Couples in the 'other's eyes' condition were significantly","w":0.83162780761718746},{"c":1,"x":0.094534257235969407,"t":"more likely to rely on wise reasoning - recognizing others' perspectives and searching for a","h":0.022917822758075856,"w":0.7842142232259115,"y":0.35962143046934192},{"y":0.3807420492020257,"t":"compromise - compared to the couples in the egocentric condition.","c":1,"w":0.58024693806966143,"h":0.01751376490149259,"x":0.092592580188024645},{"t":"'Ego-decentering promotes greater focus on others and enables a bigger picture, conceptual view","w":0.82948354085286458,"x":0.094498154086039676,"h":0.021613699527736063,"y":0.41509656204961343,"c":1},{"w":0.80468068440755203,"x":0.092522231039467606,"c":1,"t":"of the experience, affording recognition of intellectual humility and change, says Grossmann.","y":0.43284229646880767,"h":0.019930312714953047},{"h":0.021712333490486291,"t":"We might associate wisdom with intelligence or particular personality traits, but research shows","x":0.094473458275467506,"w":0.81925333658854171,"c":1,"y":0.46794152202211048},{"t":"only a small positive relationship between wise thinking and crystallized intelligence and the","w":0.7965626525878905,"h":0.02103303655437927,"c":1,"x":0.092464096470033702,"y":0.48539161917865059},{"t":"personality traits of openness and agreeableness. 'It is remarkable how much people can vary in","w":0.82121287027994794,"x":0.092486027641873345,"h":0.020572799395054631,"y":0.50358486026963956,"c":1},{"w":0.81509730021158866,"y":0.5213236810333487,"x":0.090399072244195605,"h":0.021779541132447289,"c":1,"t":"their wisdom from one situation to the next, and how much stronger such contextual effects are"},{"x":0.09247942345490183,"y":0.53920788566217759,"h":0.020443167085501535,"t":"for understanding the relationship between wise judgment and its social and affective outcomes as","w":0.83971176147460935,"c":1},{"h":0.023187605876945128,"t":"compared to the generalized \"traits\"; Grossmann explains. 'That is, knowing how wisely a person","w":0.83980326334635413,"c":1,"y":0.55531177235914042,"x":0.09245153984416106},{"h":0.023281873606399883,"c":1,"w":0.83370305379231779,"y":0.57298133409751306,"x":0.092394144247524068,"t":"behaves in a given situation is more informative for understanding their emotions or likelihood to"},{"y":0.5928466373547796,"x":0.090408683253034403,"t":"forgive [or] retaliate as compared to knowing whether the person may be wise \"in general'!","h":0.020862453537077164,"w":0.79031794230143237,"c":1}],"w":2400}